{
  "case_id": "2007-06-713",
  "document_hash": "ae3e4ca4429835068c3a9bc047777f04e1ecfe92f0415d51810b3cfcd9bbea3b",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2007-06-713",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ae3e4ca4429835068c3a9bc047777f04e1ecfe92f0415d51810b3cfcd9bbea3b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ae3e4ca44298-0000-0000",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In adopting Initiative 872, Washington's voters followed this Court's guidance in California Democratic Party v. Jones. They adopted a top-two election system. By so doing the voters eliminated the crucial constitutional defect of a partisan blanket primary because in the top-two system the voters are no longer selecting the party's nominees for the November election. The Ninth Circuit nonetheless ruled that Initiative 872 is unconstitutional, holding that allowing each candidate to state his or her personal party preference on the ballot would create the appearance of association between a political party and candidate."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Washington State Grange",
      "case_id": "2007-06-713",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ae3e4ca4429835068c3a9bc047777f04e1ecfe92f0415d51810b3cfcd9bbea3b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ae3e4ca44298-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Ninth Circuit is wrong for at least two reasons. First, the Ninth Circuit's appearance- of-association conclusion assumes that top-two ballots will look the same as ballots under a party nominating election system. They will not."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2007-06-713",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ae3e4ca4429835068c3a9bc047777f04e1ecfe92f0415d51810b3cfcd9bbea3b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ae3e4ca44298-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: But you say that the purpose of allowing a candidate to declare a preference is simply to convey useful information to voters, but once you decided, once the State decided, that the ballot was not going to indicate party affiliation, why do you limit candidates to the names of parties? Why don't you allow them to pick some other phrase that better expresses their point of view? Somebody may want to say, I'm the pro-environment candidate, or I'm the no-new-taxes candidate. Why do you limit them to saying Democrat, Republican, Libertarian, et cetera?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2007-06-713",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ae3e4ca4429835068c3a9bc047777f04e1ecfe92f0415d51810b3cfcd9bbea3b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ae3e4ca44298-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: Do you know any Democrats who go around saying I prefer the Democratic Party who do not regard themselves and register themselves as Democrats? I mean, in the real world I don't know that -- I don't know whether this is fatal to your case, but in the real world, it seems to me the distinction you're drawing is simply not drawn."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2007-06-713",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ae3e4ca4429835068c3a9bc047777f04e1ecfe92f0415d51810b3cfcd9bbea3b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ae3e4ca44298-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: No, but that isn't responsive to my question. Do you really think that that distinction is a distinction which is accepted as a working way of thinking in this world?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "State",
      "case_id": "2007-06-713",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ae3e4ca4429835068c3a9bc047777f04e1ecfe92f0415d51810b3cfcd9bbea3b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ae3e4ca44298-0001-0004",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. McKENNA: Well, the Initiative 872, Justice Kennedy, repealed the old State law which required the parties to use the State primary to select their nominees. And Initiative 872, in fact, is silent on the procedures the parties will follow. So they are left as they were back in the early days of statehood to decide for themselves how to designate their nominees."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2007-06-713",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ae3e4ca4429835068c3a9bc047777f04e1ecfe92f0415d51810b3cfcd9bbea3b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ae3e4ca44298-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: I -- I -- no, I understand that to be your position, but my question is, and I realize there is a certain awkwardness to this, but we -- we've got to face it: If the masking of the identity of the candidate is the real flaw, then the -- the hypothetical that was included in the -- in Jones, in the dictum, of the -- of the party that -- of the ballot that has no party identification whatsoever, that would equally be bad, wouldn't it?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2007-06-713",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ae3e4ca4429835068c3a9bc047777f04e1ecfe92f0415d51810b3cfcd9bbea3b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ae3e4ca44298-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: Let me ask this question: This is a facial challenge, is it not?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "District Court",
      "case_id": "2007-06-713",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ae3e4ca4429835068c3a9bc047777f04e1ecfe92f0415d51810b3cfcd9bbea3b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ae3e4ca44298-0002-0002",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The district court enjoined the entirety of Initiative 872 because it determined that the party preference provisions of Initiative 872 were not severable under Washington State's test for severability."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "State",
      "case_id": "2007-06-713",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ae3e4ca4429835068c3a9bc047777f04e1ecfe92f0415d51810b3cfcd9bbea3b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ae3e4ca44298-0002-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I -- I cannot, Your Honor, because either -- if there is legitimate State interest, the interest is in providing information about that candidate's positions and linkages to the Republican Party by saying, my preference is Republican because I believe what the Republicans are, whether that candidate is David Duke, in the Republican case, or in the case of the Democrats, a Lyndon LaRouche candidate."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2007-06-713",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ae3e4ca4429835068c3a9bc047777f04e1ecfe92f0415d51810b3cfcd9bbea3b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ae3e4ca44298-0002-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: You -- you can't answer my question? You can't hypothesize any legitimate State interest for doing that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2007-06-713",
